K11l — source-bound BPF module hardening cost, 2026-09-28
======================================================

Original bpf:test_kmod.sh still TIMEOUTs at its unchanged 300-second watchdog
on BOTH new matching Images. It completes earlier hardening modes, then starts
JIT enabled=1 / hardened=2. The unchanged source/module/budgets are not patched,
and a smaller test or longer diagnostic is never substituted for this verdict.
Original pair: .evidence/kselftest-full/20260927T220816Z/{reference,subject}/bpf/test_kmod.sh.

Reduction: the C observer loads the exact signed installed lib/test_bpf.ko with
its original test_id parameter, captures child CPU state, and unloads only after
successful initialization. The first 20-second observation of the full module
stops after completed case 850. Then eight serial single-case controls establish:
                        GCC                      EvoCC
  test_id=850 harden=1  PASS, unload              PASS, unload
  test_id=850 harden=2  PASS, unload              PASS, unload
  test_id=851 harden=1  PASS, unload              PASS, unload
  test_id=851 harden=2  TIMEOUT at 20s            TIMEOUT at 20s
The child is RUNNING and consumes system CPU, not blocked waiting for entropy.
Captures of get_random_u32 alone would not establish an entropy wait.

Causal source path (frozen Linux 6.18.44):
  lib/test_bpf.c: __bpf_fill_pattern and __bpf_fill_alu64_imm produce
    (63 * 31 * 4) + (63 * 25 * 4) = 14,112 operand pairs;
    each emits seven instructions, plus three head/tail instructions = 98,787.
  include/linux/filter.h: harden=1 bypasses constant blinding for CAP_BPF;
    harden=2 also blinds the privileged in-kernel module's program.
  kernel/bpf/core.c: bpf_jit_blind_constants patches each constant separately;
    bpf_patch_insn_single moves the remaining array and scans branch targets.
    Above S16_MAX, it scans once before expanding and once afterwards. This is
    quadratic rewriting work in these long generated patterns, before execution
    of the actual tiny semantic checks. It is not one instruction spinning.

A bounded optional kprobe observes bpf_patch_insn_single entry and its actual
position/patch width, using a four-KiB per-CPU ring (no unbounded trace files).
Independent aggregate probe counts, approximately 5s then 20s:
  GCC    12,709 -> 39,742 entries, zero missed probes; system ticks 495 -> 1981.
  EvoCC   5,827 -> 21,363 entries, zero missed probes; system ticks 466 -> 1958.
Trace positions advance; old ring entries overwrite as expected, so no complete
trace-population claim is made. Hardening=1 controls produce zero patch entries
and finish. These are diagnostic observations, not fair optimized performance
benchmarks; the EvoCC slowdown remains visible and is not waived.

Finally the SAME single case, uninstrumented, gets a separately declared
180-second observer limit (200-second init watchdog, 260-second boot bound).
Both initialize, report "Summary: 1 PASSED, 0 FAILED, [1/1 JIT'ed]", unload and
power off. Kernel timestamps from case entry to summary are approximately
43.524s for GCC and 92.786s for EvoCC. These prove eventual completion of this
one original case, not the full thousand-case/four-mode program within 300s.
Changing that program's budget/source would require a separately labelled
configuration. No compiler or kernel production patch follows from this test.

Reproduction uses the existing selftest-init/boot/flight helpers, sealed GCC14
-static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror observer builds, original signed modules and
the existing 64 MiB snapshot scratch disk. For single-case builds define
TEST_PARAMETERS="test_id=850" or "test_id=851", TEST_HARDENING="1" or "2";
TEST_PROFILE=1 enables bounded probes; TEST_BUDGET_SECONDS=180 selects only the
explicit completion experiment. All inner verdicts and boot nonces are parsed.
An initial missing collection selector correctly failed the parser because it
ran two programs while expecting one; that setup attempt remains, not PASS.

